CH = dict(num=12, tag="Civics (Part 2)", theme="Citizenship, Fundamental Rights, Fundamental Duties, discrimination and inclusion",
 title="Citizenship: Rights and Duties",
 intro="Standing up for a classmate who is teased, building a ramp for a friend in a wheelchair, keeping a colony clean: these are everyday acts of citizenship. This chapter explains who a citizen is, which rights the Constitution guarantees, which duties we owe in return, and how to resist discrimination and build an inclusive society.",
 big_questions=["What does it mean to be a citizen, not just legally but in everyday life?", "Why are both rights and duties essential?", "How can we build a society where everyone is treated fairly, respected and included?"],
 blocks=[
 ("h2", "Who Is a Citizen?"),
 ("note", "DEFINITION", "A **citizen** is a person who legally belongs to a country and is a member of its political community, so can take part in its decision-making. Citizenship is both a **legal identity** and a **social responsibility**."),
 ("table", ["", "Resident", "Citizen"], [["Meaning", "Lives in a place", "Formal legal member of the country"], ["Rights", "Not all rights of a citizen", "Rights and duties under the Constitution"], ["Example", "A foreigner working in India", "An Indian who lives abroad is still an Indian citizen"]]),
 ("ul", ["Citizens enjoy rights such as voting, free speech and living and working safely, but these rights have **limits**.", "They also have duties: obeying the law, paying taxes, respecting others and helping keep society safe.", "All citizens are **equal under the Constitution**; the rules for gaining or losing citizenship come from the **Citizenship Act, 1955** and the **Citizenship (Amendment) Act, 2019**."]),
 ("p", "The chapter studies four ideas: **rights, duties, discrimination** and **inclusion**. Inclusion is the positive way of ensuring non-discrimination."),
 ("h2", "Rights"),
 ("note", "DEFINITION", "**Rights** are justified claims that we need to live with dignity, safety and respect, such as education, livelihood and free expression. Actions that harm ourselves or others cannot be called rights."),
 ("h3", "The six Fundamental Rights"),
 ("table", ["Fundamental Right", "Articles", "What it protects"], [["Right to equality", "14–18", "Equality before law; no discrimination; equal opportunity in public jobs; end of untouchability and titles (except military and academic)"], ["Right to freedom", "19–22", "Speech, movement, peaceful assembly; life and personal liberty; education (21A)"], ["Right against exploitation", "23–24", "Bans trafficking, forced labour and child labour"], ["Right to freedom of religion", "25–28", "Practise, profess and manage religion; no forced religion"], ["Cultural and educational rights", "29–30", "Language, script and culture; minority institutions"], ["Right to constitutional remedies", "32", "Approach the Supreme Court if rights are violated"]]),
 ("note", "IMPORTANT", "Fundamental Rights protect people from unfair treatment by others **and** from unfair actions of the government. They are **enforceable by courts**."),
 ("h3", "Equality and equity"),
 ("ul", ["**Equality** gives everyone the same support; **equity** gives each person the support they need, because historical inequality makes people start differently.", "Example: the **Beti Bachao Beti Padhao** campaign raises awareness and supports girls’ education.", "Ramps for wheelchairs and braille books are equity measures."]),
 ("h3", "Freedom, life and dignity"),
 ("ul", ["The **right to freedom** covers speech, movement and peaceful assembly, but with **reasonable restrictions** so freedoms are not misused.", "The **right to life** means living with dignity: livelihood, education, health, a clean environment and fair treatment. It also protects children with disabilities.", "**Rights of nature:** New Zealand treats the Whanganui River as a living being and Costa Rica protects Nature’s rights. In 2017 the Uttarakhand High Court gave the Ganga and Yamuna legal status, but the Supreme Court overturned it as legally unsustainable."]),
 ("h3", "Exploitation, religion, culture and remedies"),
 ("ul", ["**Exploitation** means taking unfair advantage of others: bonded labour, human trafficking, abuse and child labour. Children below **14** may not be employed in factories, mines or hazardous work; they may help in family work after school or in vacations.", "**Childline 1098** is the national helpline for children (remember: count backwards from 10). In 2015 a call led to the rescue of 20 children from a clothes-embroidery factory.", "The **right to freedom of religion** lets everyone choose, practise and share their religion; no one is forced to follow or fund one, and government schools cannot promote a religion.", "**Cultural and educational rights**: communities can protect language and traditions; no student can be refused admission on grounds of religion, caste, race or language; minorities can run their own schools.", "**Right to constitutional remedies** (Article 32): Dr B. R. Ambedkar called it the *soul and heart* of the Constitution because rights would otherwise be only promises on paper."]),
 ("h2", "Duties"),
 ("note", "DEFINITION", "**Fundamental Duties** are responsibilities of citizens listed in **Article 51A (Part IV-A)**. They remind us that being a citizen is not only enjoying rights but also contributing to the nation."),
 ("ul", ["The original Constitution (1950) had no duties. **10 duties were added in 1976** by an amendment.", "The **11th duty** was added in **2002**: parents or guardians must ensure education for children aged **6 to 14**."]),
 ("table", ["Duty (summary)", "Example from the chapter"], [["Respect the Constitution, Flag and Anthem", "Standing silently during the anthem"], ["Follow the ideals of the freedom struggle", "Speaking up against bullying"], ["Protect India’s unity, integrity and sovereignty", "Friends learning each other’s languages"], ["Defend the nation and help in emergencies", "NCC cadets in a flood"], ["Promote harmony and respect women", "Stopping students who tease a girl"], ["Preserve culture and heritage", "Not writing on a fort wall"], ["Protect the environment and show compassion to animals", "Planting trees; water bowls for strays"], ["Develop scientific temper", "Teaching basic water filtering"], ["Protect public property and avoid violence", "Not breaking benches after a defeat"], ["Strive for excellence", "Waste segregation in school"], ["Ensure education for children aged 6–14", "Persuading a father to send his daughter to school"]]),
 ("note", "NOTE", "Ancient ideas: **dharma** (duty and what benefits society), **ṛiṇa** (debts to gods, ancestors, teachers, fellow humans and Nature) and **adhikāra** (capacity or authority, later meaning rights)."),
 ("h2", "Rights and Duties: Two Sides of the Same Coin"),
 ("table", ["", "Rights", "Duties"], [["Nature", "Freedoms and benefits every citizen enjoys", "Responsibilities towards others"], ["Enforcement", "Protected by law; courts can enforce", "Moral and social; not always legally punished"], ["Purpose", "Live with dignity", "Use freedom wisely and respectfully"]]),
 ("ul", ["Every right has a matching duty: the right to respect brings the duty to respect others; the right to express opinions brings the duty to listen.", "Right to education matches the parents’ duty to educate and the student’s duty to learn."]),
 ("h2", "Discrimination"),
 ("note", "DEFINITION", "**Discrimination** is unfair treatment of a person or group because of caste, religion, ethnicity, disability, race, appearance, gender, sexuality or economic background. It is unethical and legally prohibited."),
 ("ul", ["It harms the person psychologically and physically and also society; it creates barriers that stop people from enjoying Fundamental Rights.", "**Visible**: teasing Rohan for his weight. **Invisible**: ignoring Mina because her family could not afford a trip. Invisible discrimination is hard to prove and so hard to punish.", "**Savitribai Phule** opened a school for girls in the mid-19th century despite abuse; **Anandibai Joshi**, married at nine in 1874, became a doctor against social opposition."]),
 ("h2", "Inclusion"),
 ("note", "DEFINITION", "**Inclusion** means creating an environment where everyone feels welcomed, respected and valued, with equal access to opportunities, especially groups historically excluded."),
 ("ul", ["It goes beyond reservations: language support, welcoming all castes and religions, braille books, ramps and classroom help.", "Example: Smt. Amita’s reading club added regional-language books so that all children joined.", "Students can include others by waiting, explaining patiently and giving everyone a chance to speak."]),
 ("qa", [
  ("Who is a citizen?", "A person who legally belongs to a country and can take part in its decision-making."),
  ("How many categories of Fundamental Rights are there?", "Six, from equality to constitutional remedies."),
  ("Difference between equality and equity?", "Equality gives the same support; equity gives support according to need."),
  ("Which article protects children’s free and compulsory education?", "Article 21A, for ages 6 to 14."),
  ("When were Fundamental Duties added?", "In 1976; the 11th duty was added in 2002."),
  ("Why is Article 32 important?", "It lets citizens go to the Supreme Court to enforce their rights."),
  ("Why are rights and duties linked?", "Each right requires others to be respected, so rights come with matching duties."),
  ("What is invisible discrimination?", "Indirect unfair treatment such as exclusion or jibes; it is hard to prove.")]),
 ],
 takeaways=["A citizen has **legal membership** of a country, with **rights and duties**.", "The Constitution gives **six Fundamental Rights**, enforceable through courts (Article 32).", "**Fundamental Duties (Article 51A)** were added in 1976; the 11th in 2002.", "**Rights and duties** go together: use freedom wisely and respect others.", "**Discrimination** violates the Constitution; **inclusion** removes barriers and makes everyone feel welcome.", "Everyday actions such as speaking up, following the law and including others build a peaceful society."])
